SOLUTIONS={
1:('DIG-01','In 2015, the 1 occupies the tens column. It represents one ten, or 10.'),
2:('RATE-01','Ten twenty-cent coins total 10 × 20 = 200 cents. Since 100 cents is one dollar, that is $2.'),
3:('DATA-03','The large marks are five degrees apart and each small interval is one degree. The liquid stops three small intervals above 25°C, at 28°C.'),
4:('ALG-03','Increasing 19 to 20 adds one. To keep the total unchanged, reduce 45 by one to 44. Check: 19 + 45 = 64 = 20 + 44.'),
5:('DIG-01','Align the decimal places: 1.300, 1.303, 1.310, 1.301 and 1.131. The largest is 1.310, written 1.31. More decimal digits do not necessarily mean a larger value.'),
6:('GEO-01','Each shape contains five unit squares, so begin with 5 × 4 = 20 separate edges. Every shared side removes two from the outside perimeter. B has five shared sides, giving 20 − 10 = 10 units. Each other shape has only four shared sides and perimeter 12. Thus B is smallest.'),
7:('FRAC-01','The whole array has 10 × 12 = 120 dinosaurs, and half is 60. Simon’s 6 × 10, Carrie’s 5 × 12, Brian’s 10 × 12 ÷ 2, and Rémy’s 10 ÷ 2 × 12 all equal 60. All four methods are correct.'),
8:('LOGIC-03','The centre belongs to both sums, so first make the two outer pairs equal. Put 5 on the left and 9 at the bottom: 5 + 7 = 3 + 9 = 12. The remaining number 1 goes in the centre, giving both sums 13. This is the offered possible total.'),
9:('LOGIC-03','The row counts are 3,1,2,3 and the column counts are 2,2,4,1. A counter at A increases the first row to 4 and the first column to 3. The row counts become 4,1,2,3 and columns become 3,2,4,1: all different within each list.'),
10:('FRAC-01','If one-third of a number is 1/2, then three such thirds make the whole number: 3 × 1/2 = 3/2 = 1½.'),
11:('GEO-04','Each fold halves the current right-isosceles triangle, so three folds create eight equal triangular regions when unfolded. Reverse the folds and reflect every crease across the previous crease. The resulting symmetric eight-region pattern is B. The alternatives either omit reflected creases or add folds that were not made.'),
12:('FRAC-01','Calculate the five values: L + M = 100.1; L × M = 10; L ÷ M = 1000; M ÷ L = 0.001; L − M = 99.9. Dividing by one-tenth multiplies by ten, so L ÷ M is largest.'),
13:('SPATIAL-03','Count areas to identify the possible rectangle, then check the corner shape. D and the shaded piece each contain four squares, so together they would have to form a 2-by-4 rectangle (a 1-by-8 rectangle is impossible because the pieces are wider than one square). The missing corner shape needed beside D is the mirror image of the shaded piece, not a rotation of it. Since flipping is forbidden, D cannot work. The other pieces fit by rotations and slides.'),
14:('GEO-05','Imagine joining the centres of the outer pipes with a taut polygon. The curved parts of the rope add up to the same full circle in every arrangement, so compare only the straight portions. In pipe-diameter units, A,D,E have straight total 10, B has 12, and the compact hexagonal arrangement C has 9. Thus C needs the shortest rope.'),
15:('COUNT-05','Adding the three side totals gives 30. The six numbers themselves total 21, but each corner is counted twice while each middle circle is counted once. Therefore the three corners total 30 − 21 = 9. One corner is 1, so the other two total 8. The shaded circle lies between those two on a side totalling 10, so it must be 10 − 8 = 2.'),
16:('SEQ-03','Follow the loop carefully: start with 5, subtract 2 to get 3, then multiply by 3 to get 9. Repeat: (9 − 2) × 3 = 21. Repeat again: (21 − 2) × 3 = 57. This is the first result greater than 50, so the selected answer is 57.'),
17:('GEO-04','The two top corners are folded inward. The horizontal cut crosses both folded triangles. Reflect that cut back across each diagonal fold line: the reflected portions become vertical cuts in the original paper. Together with the central horizontal cut they remove a rectangular notch from the top edge. The unfolded outline is D.'),
18:('COUNT-06','Number Sunday through Saturday as 0 through 6. The two gaps must be at least 2 days, with the second larger. The possible triples are (0,2,5), (0,2,6), (0,3,6) and (1,3,6). None includes 4, which is Thursday. Every other offered weekday occurs in at least one valid triple.'),
19:('GEO-02','In the small square, the shaded triangle is half of 3 × 3, giving 4.5 cm². In the large square, the unshaded triangle has base 5 and height 5 − 3 = 2, so its area is 5 cm². Its shaded area is 25 − 5 = 20 cm². Total shaded area: 4.5 + 20 = 24.5 cm².'),
20:('SOLID-03','Keep the M face fixed while comparing the given views. A moves from the top to the left; T replaces it on top, so T is opposite C. This immediately rules out views containing both C and T. Now track the printed letters as the cube rotates, not merely which letters touch. Turning the second cube to put M on the left allows the hidden H face to appear on the right with T still above, exactly as in A. The remaining views require a printed face to be twisted relative to its neighbours. Thus A is a possible third view.'),
21:('LOGIC-01','An odd product means all three numbers are odd. The only sets of three different positive odd numbers summing to 13 are {1,3,9} and {1,5,7}. A student holding 3,9,5 or 7 can identify the whole set immediately. A student holding 1 cannot distinguish the two sets. Therefore Asha has 1. Zero cannot occur because it would make the product even.'),
22:('DIG-05','The units column requires M² to end in M. Since the product has four digits, M cannot be 0 or 1, leaving 5 or 6. If M = 5, its carry of 2 makes the tens digit of 5L + 2 either 2 or 7, never 5. Thus M = 6. The carry is 3, and 6L + 3 must end in 5, giving L = 2 or 7. Testing gives 226 × 6 = 1356 (wrong hundreds digit) and 776 × 6 = 4656. Hence L = 7, M = 6, N = 4 and their sum is 17.'),
23:('FRAC-02','Before the added metals, the piece weighed 660 − 120 − 40 = 500 g. A 2:3 ratio divides this into 200 g copper and 300 g zinc. After addition the amounts are 320 g and 340 g. Divide both by 20 to simplify: copper:zinc = 16:17.'),
24:('DIV-02','One less than the card total is divisible by 4,5 and 6, hence by their least common multiple 60. Between 50 and 200 the possible totals are 61,121 and 181. Only 121 is a square: 11 × 11. There are 11 cards on each side.'),
25:('ALG-03','Every replacement doubles a note’s value except $20 → $50, which gives $10 extra beyond doubling. Doubling the original $400 would give $800; the actual $900 contains $100 extra. Thus there were 100 ÷ 10 = 10 original $20 notes. They become ten $50 notes, worth $500.')}
